i am raghavindran bioinformatics mentor  and research consultant with omix logic  let me brief you a little bit about my  background on research interests i am a  computational biologist by training and  i specialize in the subjects like  multi-omics data analysis and data  science methods in precision oncology  and protein social bioinformatics i  completed my formal education in  biophysics and bioinformatics and then  graduated phd in computational biology  from max bank institute germany then  i worked on projects in the institute of  bioinformatics and applied biotechnology  in bengaluru and java media university  as a postdoctoral research scientist  over throughout my research career i  developed special interests  and expanded my expertise in precision  anthology through data science methods  that is applied to several multi-org  data so right throughout this career  right from my phd period i have had the  fortunate  experience to mentor  different undergraduate students and  enjoy the experience immensely so i feel  very much privileged to work with some  of the bright young minds like you all  in the past and also in the future i  wish and i hope you all to enjoy as much  as i do in this partnership between  amity noita and fine biotech university  now let me briefly  speak about one of my research projects  on precision oncology so i just  okay it's doing okay i chose to speak uh  specifically about this study because it  utilizes a pipeline of very well  established multi-omics data analysis  methods on publicly available data so i  hope at the end of this uh  a small dem  talk  this project will inspire you all to  develop your own scientific curiosity  leveraging the abundance of data that  are publicly available  so uh  i think you all had a very good  introduction about what cancer is and  how that is the leading cause of deaths  disease deaths worldwide so for such a  dreadful disease a systematic cancer  treatments have relied on  chemotherapeutic drugs that are  marginally more toxic to cancer cells  than normal cells  this is a huge problem  because on an average only 25 percent of  the cancer patients responds to cancer  treatments and in comparison about 80  percent of the patients will respond  positively to popular painkillers that  are available in the market so a  prominent solution to this problem is  precision oncological therapy where a  treatment strategy is designed by  targeting the molecular characteristics  of the patient's tumor  hence the treatment is personalized to  the patients who are likely to respond  positively to specific cancer therapies  unfortunately there is not enough data  from cancer patients to molecularly  characterize all the tumors under  multitude of different treatment options  hence we have to resort to a suitable  model system to predict tumor responses  to several treatments  so we have already covered why we have  to do this and let me straight away go  to how  the tumor cells from cancer patients are  subjected to next-gen sequencing  techniques and the information about the  gene expression gene mutation and  epigenetic variation were extracted this  data molecularly characterizes the  tumors these tumor samples in the are  then grafted into immunosuppressed mouse  and administered several cancer drugs  then the treat the tumors response to  these respective drugs are documented  this data tells us about the tumor's  reaction to different treatments  both the molecular characterization data  and the treatments response data are  available along with the publication  such a wealth of data generates a  plethora of research questions  i use this data to design and implement  machine learning models to predict tumor  responses to different treatments that  are available  so what did we find we found that the  treatment response could be predicted  more accurately when we use data from  multiple genes compared to traditional  single gene biomarkers and the accuracy  of such prediction can be increased  immensely with more than one type of  sequencing data such as the gene  expression or the gene mutation or the  copy number variation data was used to  train the machine learning models  so that is what is depicted here as we  go from left to right the information  content increases first this method uses  only a single gene biomarker and the  accuracy which which  is supposed to be close to one is  scattered all around the scale but when  we increase the information content to  multiple genes and supplement that with  several other  sequencing data like gene mutation data  and copy number variation data the  accuracy tremendously increases and the  consistency also increases so what more  could be done with this data a gene set  enrichment analysis  on the on the set of most informative  genes from these models can be performed  to identify specific biomarkers for the  tumors response to different  treatment regimes  one can also extend such an analysis to  include patient data from the tcga  repository and these yeah  these supports are currently carried out  are being carried out by my partners in  the jain year so i hope this was very  informative and inspiring for you all to  generate interesting research questions  on the data that are publicly available  so i see that you had an excellent  introduction to the platform that is  comics logics and now i want to  provide you a sample of how the lessons  involving coding is organized on this  platform the courses that involve coding  a small assembly of courses that are  involved coding are given in this slide  and they're all designed and directed  specifically towards students who have  completed the introductory courses in  their respective subjects so all these  courses have the following structure and  in the part that involve executing r or  python codes so these lessons include  what analysis are we doing why are we  doing such an analysis  and what do we expect to learn from such  an analysis and finally how to do such  an analysis the codes themselves as  given in as given in an example here  are include themselves include  sufficient explanatory comments at each  step so it is very easy to follow and  finally when the output is generated  from performing such an analysis  and we also give and we also give a  brief uh account on how to interpret  this analysis or how to interpret the  results of such an analysis with respect  to the biological question at hand  so  this is how the coding lessons involving  coding are handled in the  platform that you'll be are about to  enjoy  so so many students congregating at one  place would naturally precipitate into  even into an evolution of interesting  science student clubs so sciomics club  is one such initiative taken by all the  students associated with time biotech  the goal of this club is to help fellow  students to to learn about bioinformatic  research using our via group sessions  and easy to take courses and debates and  discussion this club welcomes students  from all the fields of life sciences and  is not limited to anybody who has an  introduction to bioinformatics or any  such computational biology so  these students will meet together and  learn from and with each other so they  have this this club has definitive  topics of interest every month and based  on this topic two guest speakers are  invited to talk on the topics of the  month which is then followed by short q  a focused on the role of bioinformatics  and some carrier prospects for today's  students and there are specific  instructions on how to join and be part  of this club in the omixlogic platform  website and overall  i would like you all to know that i will  be talking in detail as a guest speaker  on this club this saturday that is  november 20. i
 

